\begin{abstract}
We study the response equivalence of edges in the quantum graph Heisenberg model on all connected graphs with $N=6$ (112 graphs) and $N=7$ (853 graphs) nodes. Same orbit implies same response with zero counterexamples for both scales. Different orbits can also give the same response: 47 pairs for $N=6$ and 382 pairs for $N=7$, not generated by involutive automorphisms. The mechanism is a layered quantum resonance. Layer 1: total spin equality, $S_1 = S_2$ (47/47 recall, zero false negatives). Layer 2: absolute coupling equality at $s=1/2$, $|sd_1(1/2)| = |sd_2(1/2)|$ (47/47 recall). Layer 3: the sign rule is determined by $S$: $+$ for $S=0$, $-$ for $S=1$ (47/47 recall). Layer 4: absolute coupling equality over the window $s \in [-1,1]$, $|sd_1(s)| = |sd_2(s)|$, selecting 38 pairs with $100\%$ precision and $80.9\%$ recall. Layer 5: pair-level window boundary. The maximal-window right boundary $s_{\max}$ clusters by graph but is not strictly a graph-level constant. Observed values: $-0.05$ for graph 38 (6 pairs) and for one pair of graph 60; $0.95$ for the other two pairs of graph 60; $1.00$ for graph 103 (3 pairs); and $\ge 1.45$ for all other contributing graphs. No single window achieves both high precision and high recall. The cross-scale invariant is the resonance condition $p_S = 1/2$: the non-degenerate $-1$ hit rate is $94.7\%$ for both $N=6$ ($18/19$) and $N=7$ ($7486/7903$). For degenerate ground states, the $-1$ hit rate is $65.5\%$ for $N=6$ (integer-spin degeneracies) and $38.0\%$ for $N=7$ (half-integer-spin degeneracies). All data are reproducible.
\end{abstract}

