\section{Model and Definitions}

\subsection{Hamiltonian}

We use the isotropic Heisenberg model:
\begin{equation}
H = \sum_{(i,j)\in E} J_{ij}\left(\sigma_i^x\sigma_j^x + \sigma_i^y\sigma_j^y + \sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z\right),
\end{equation}
where the spin operators use the $S = \sigma$ convention. One edge, the defect edge, has coupling $s$. All other edges have coupling $1.0$. We work in the $S_z=0$ subspace. We use exact diagonalization. When the ground state is degenerate, we use the symmetry-reduced density matrix.

The choice $S = \sigma$ is a normalization. Earlier work with $S = \sigma/2$ gave gaps half as large and missed the values $-2$ and $1-\sqrt{3}$. The physical conclusions are unchanged.

\subsection{Fine-Grained Diagnostic}

For a given $s$, the fine-grained diagnostic is
\begin{equation}
E(s) = \operatorname{std}\left\{\langle\sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z\rangle_s\right\}_{(i,j)\in E}.
\end{equation}
This is the standard deviation of all edge correlation functions. It measures the dispersion of the correlation distribution. It is a relational observable: it depends on differences between edges, not on absolute values.

\subsection{Sorted Correlation Distribution}

At each $s$, we sort all edge correlation values. We obtain the sorted correlation distribution curve
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_e(s) = \operatorname{sort}\left\{\langle\sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z\rangle_s^{(e)}\right\}_{(i,j)\in E},
\end{equation}
where the superscript $(e)$ means edge $e$ is the defect edge.

\subsection{Response Equivalence}

Two edges $e_1$ and $e_2$ are response-equivalent if and only if
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e_1}(s) = \mathcal{R}_{e_2}(s)
\end{equation}
for all $s$ (tolerance $10^{-10}$).

\subsection{Two-Site Reduced Density Matrix and the Singlet Probability}

For a defect edge $e=(i,j)$, we define the two-site reduced density matrix
\begin{equation}
\rho_{ij} = \operatorname{Tr}_{\bar{ij}}\left(\rho_{\text{GS}}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\rho_{\text{GS}}$ is the ground-state density matrix (symmetry-reduced if degenerate) and the trace is over all sites except $i$ and $j$. The singlet probability is
\begin{equation}
p_S = \langle S | \rho_{ij} | S \rangle, \quad |S\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle - |\downarrow\uparrow\rangle\right).
\end{equation}
For an SU(2)-symmetric ground state, the reduced density matrix takes the form
\begin{equation}
\rho_{ij} = p_S |S\rangle\langle S| + \frac{1-p_S}{3}\left(|T_+\rangle\langle T_+| + |T_0\rangle\langle T_0| + |T_-\rangle\langle T_-\right),
\end{equation}
with a single scalar $p_S$. The defect-edge correlation is
\begin{equation}
\langle\sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z\rangle = \frac{1}{3} - \frac{4 p_S}{3},
\end{equation}
and the Heisenberg coupling expectation is
\begin{equation}
\langle \boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}_j \rangle = 1 - 4 p_S.
\end{equation}
This is the single-parameter characterization of the defect-edge state.

\subsection{Per-Pair Window Boundary}

For a pair of edges $(e_1, e_2)$ in a graph $G$, we define the maximal window $W(e_1, e_2) = [s_{\min}, s_{\max}]$ as the largest connected interval in $s$ over which $|sd_1(s)| = |sd_2(s)|$ holds within numerical tolerance. The right boundary $s_{\max}$ clusters by graph but is not strictly a graph-level constant: different pairs within the same graph may have different $s_{\max}$ if their $sd$ curves diverge at different $s$ values. Graph 60 provides an explicit example: two of its three pairs have $s_{\max} = 0.95$, the third has $s_{\max} = -0.05$.

\subsection{Involutive Automorphism}

Let $\phi$ be a node automorphism of $G$. $\phi$ is an involution if $\phi\circ\phi = \mathrm{id}$. It is non-trivial if $\phi(v) \neq v$ for some $v$. An involution induces a pairing on edges: if $\phi(e_1) = e_2$ and $e_1 \neq e_2$, then $e_1$ and $e_2$ are a pair of involution images.

\subsection{Ground-State Degeneracy}

When the ground state is degenerate, a single eigenvector does not respect the automorphism symmetry. We use the symmetry-reduced density matrix:
\begin{equation}
\rho_{\text{GS}} = \frac{1}{d}\sum_{k=1}^{d}|\psi_k\rangle\langle\psi_k|,
\end{equation}
where $d$ is the degeneracy. Correlations are $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_{\text{GS}}\,\sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z)$.

For $K_6$, the contrast is shown in Table~\ref{tab:deg}. A single eigenvector gives 105 same-orbit different-curve pairs. The symmetry-reduced density matrix gives zero. The explicit treatment of degeneracy is essential.

