\subsection{Mechanism for Non-Degenerate Ground States: SU(2) Singlet-Probability Resonance}

\subsubsection{Step 1: SU(2) Symmetry and the Single-Parameter Theorem}

The Heisenberg Hamiltonian is SU(2) symmetric. The ground state of a connected Heisenberg graph with even $N$ is a total-spin singlet. We verify this numerically: for all $4293$ sampling points $(gid, \text{defect}, s)$ across the 47 same-response pairs, the Pauli correlations satisfy
\begin{equation}
\langle \sigma_i^x \sigma_j^x \rangle = \langle \sigma_i^y \sigma_j^y \rangle = \langle \sigma_i^z \sigma_j^z \rangle,
\end{equation}
with zero exceptions. The two-site reduced density matrix $\rho_{ij}$ therefore takes the form
\begin{equation}
\rho_{ij} = p_S |S\rangle\langle S| + \frac{1-p_S}{3}\left(|T_+\rangle\langle T_+| + |T_0\rangle\langle T_0| + |T_-\rangle\langle T_-\right),
\end{equation}
with a single scalar $p_S$. This is the single-parameter theorem.

\subsubsection{Step 2: Resonance as an Exact Algebraic Value of $p_S$}

The defect-edge correlation function locks onto exact algebraic values when $p_S$ reaches specific points:
\begin{align}
p_S = 0 &\iff \langle\boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}_j\rangle = +1, \\
p_S = \frac{1}{2} &\iff \langle\boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}_j\rangle = -1, \\
p_S = 1 &\iff \langle\boldsymbol{\sigma}_i \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}_j\rangle = -3.
\end{align}
In the 81-point scan of $s \in [-2.0, 2.0]$ across the 47 same-response pairs, the value $+1/3$ appears at $1606/4293 = 37.41\%$ with numerical precision $10^{-16}$. This establishes that $+1/3$ is an exact algebraic number.

