\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Relationship with Standard Physics}

The result ``same orbit implies same response'' is a direct consequence of graph automorphism symmetry. This work verifies it with zero counterexamples on all connected graphs with $N=6$ and $N=7$, for all four interaction rules, and delineates its boundary.

The mechanism is resolved as a five-layer quantum resonance. Layer 1 ($S_1 = S_2$) is universal. Layer 2 ($|sd_1(1/2)| = |sd_2(1/2)|$) holds for both $S$ branches. Layer 3 (sign rule) is determined by the total spin. Layer 4 ($|sd_1(s)| = |sd_2(s)|$ for $s \in [-1,1]$) is the strongest fixed-window sufficient condition, with $100\%$ precision and $80.9\%$ recall. Layer 5 is the pair-level window boundary $s_{\max}$, which clusters by graph but is not strictly a graph-level constant. This layered picture explains why no single graph-theoretic condition could discriminate the core layer: the mechanism is quantum, not graph-theoretic.

\subsection{Relationship with Relational Ontology}

Relational ontology states that the properties of an object are determined by its position in the relational network. Response equivalence is a concrete realization. The involution is a global symmetry of the relational field.

The different-orbit same-response pairs show that the relational position is not fully captured by the automorphism orbit. The mechanism is a layered quantum resonance: total spin equality, absolute coupling equality, sign rule, and pair-level window boundary. The pair-level window boundary $s_{\max}$ is a fingerprint of the pair's relation position.

\section{Limitations and Open Problems}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Finite size: exhaustive verification for $N=6$ and $N=7$. $N=8$ requires full enumeration.
\item Analytic derivation of the $p_S(s)$ curve: the remaining open problem is the analytic derivation of $p_S(s)$.
\item Pair-level window boundary: the right boundary $s_{\max}$ clusters by graph but is not strictly a graph-level constant. Graph 60 has two pairs with $s_{\max} = 0.95$ and one pair with $s_{\max} = -0.05$. The number of bridges is not a consistent predictor of $s_{\max}$. The exact relation between $s_{\max}$ and the pair's relation position requires further analysis.
\item Core layer: no graph-theoretic quantity discriminates the 24 core pairs. The core and middle layers are distinguished by the $p_S$-curve height.
\item Degenerate ground states: the $-1$ hit rate drops from $65.5\%$ ($N=6$) to $38.0\%$ ($N=7$). A complete mechanism remains open.
\item Model dependence: Ising has 493 different-orbit same-response pairs, likely degeneracy artifacts.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For all 112 connected graphs with $N=6$ and all 853 connected graphs with $N=7$ in the quantum Heisenberg model:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Same orbit implies same response: zero counterexamples for $N=6$ and $N=7$, and for all four interaction rules.
\item Different orbits can give the same response: 47 pairs for $N=6$, 382 pairs for $N=7$.
\item The mechanism for the 47 pairs is a five-layer quantum resonance. Layer 1: $S_1 = S_2$ (47/47 recall). Layer 2: $|sd_1(1/2)| = |sd_2(1/2)|$ (47/47 recall). Layer 3: sign rule determined by $S$ (47/47 recall). Layer 4: $|sd_1(s)| = |sd_2(s)|$ for $s \in [-1,1]$ (38/38 precision, 38/47 recall). Layer 5: pair-level window boundary $s_{\max}$, which clusters by graph.
\item No single window achieves both high precision and high recall. The window $[-1,1]$ gives $95.0\%$ precision and $80.9\%$ recall; the window $[-2,-0.5]$ gives $24.0\%$ precision and $100\%$ recall.
\item The cross-scale invariant is the resonance condition $p_S = 1/2$. The non-degenerate $-1$ hit rate is $94.7\%$ for both $N=6$ and $N=7$.
\item For the core layer of 24 pairs, no graph-theoretic quantity discriminates it. The core and middle layers are distinguished by the $p_S$-curve height.
\item For degenerate ground states, the $-1$ hit rate is $65.5\%$ for $N=6$ and $38.0\%$ for $N=7$. A complete mechanism remains open.
\end{enumerate}
The boundary of the data is drawn. Same orbit implies same response. Different orbits may also give the same response, through a five-layer quantum resonance mechanism with a pair-level relation-position fingerprint.

